<h1>Low-Profile Conveyors: Compact &amp; Knife-Edge Designs</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Low-profile conveyors minimize overall height to fit under equipment, integrate at ergonomic workstation heights, and enable transfers between closely spaced machines. With overall heights as low as 1-2 inches, these compact <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyors</a> use thin belt designs, small-diameter nose bars, and compact drives to maximize vertical clearance. Knife-edge versions enable near-zero-gap product transfers.</p>
<h2 id="features-heading">Low-Profile Design Elements</h2>
<h3>Minimal Frame Height</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors use thin extruded aluminum or sheet metal frames rather than bulky structural sections. Frame heights of 1-3 inches are typical. The entire conveyor assembly (frame + belt + drive) may be only 2-4 inches tall. Some designs use a "flat" frame that positions the belt flush with the top surface.</p>
<h3>Small-Diameter Pulleys</h3>
<p>End pulleys (nose bars) as small as 1/4" to 1" diameter reduce the conveyor's end height. Standard conveyors use 2-4" pulleys; low-profile designs use 1/2" to 1" typical. Smaller pulleys require belts designed for tight bend radii (timing belts, thin urethane belts).</p>
<h3>Knife-Edge Transfer</h3>
<p>A knife-edge nose bar (1/4" to 3/4" diameter) creates an extremely sharp discharge point, minimizing the gap between the conveyor and downstream equipment. Products transition smoothly with no dead zone where small items could fall. Essential for transferring small bottles, vials, cartons, and blister packs.</p>
<h3>End Drive vs. Center Drive</h3>
<p><strong>End drive</strong> places the motor at one end, adding to overall length but keeping the profile thin. <strong>Center/under-belt drive</strong> mounts the motor below the belt, maintaining a flat profile but adding height. Choose based on whether length or height is the critical constraint.</p>
<h3>Belt Selection</h3>
<p>Low-profile conveyors require belts that handle tight pulley diameters without damage:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timing belts:</strong> Positive drive, tight radius capability, precise positioning</li>
<li><strong>Thin urethane belts:</strong> 1-2mm thick, flexible, good grip</li>
<li><strong>Flat belts:</strong> Thin polyester or nylon carcass</li>
<li><strong>Round belts:</strong> Multiple parallel round belts for light loads</li>
</ul>
