<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a low-profile conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A low-profile conveyor has minimal overall height, typically 2-4 inches from floor to conveying surface. Low-profile conveyors fit under equipment, integrate at ergonomic heights, and enable transfers between closely spaced machines using thin <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt designs</a>, small-diameter nose bars, and compact drive systems.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a knife-edge conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A knife-edge conveyor uses an extremely small diameter nose bar (1/4" to 3/4") at the discharge end, creating a sharp transition for minimal-gap product transfers. The knife-edge prevents small products from falling into the gap between conveyors. Common in <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a>, electronics, and small parts packaging.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How thin can a conveyor be?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The thinnest conveyors have overall heights of 1-2 inches using knife-edge nose bars and thin timing or urethane belts. Standard low-profile conveyors are 2-4 inches tall. Minimum height depends on belt type, product weight, speed, and whether the conveyor uses end-drive or under-belt drive configuration.</p>

</div>
